<|eom|><|start|>assistant to=user<|message|>In PL/I an array is introduced with a DCL statement. The ARRAY keyword may be used
explicitly and the declaration is

    DCL ARRAY <array-name> (<dimension-spec>) <data-attributes>

where <dimension-spec> is the subscript range(s) of the array and <data-attributes>
is the PL/I data type and its attributes.

* 1-dimensional

    DCL SALARY ARRAY(50) FIXED DEC(7,2);

declares SALARY as an array of 50 fixed decimal items with 7 digits, 2 after the
point.  The same declaration can be written in the more usual short form

    DCL SALARY(50) FIXED DEC(7,2);

* 2-dimensional

    DCL MATRIX ARRAY(10,20) FLOAT BIN(53);

declares MATRIX as a 10 by 20 array of floating point binary items.

* Character array

    DCL NAME ARRAY(100) CHAR(20);

declares NAME as an array of 100 character strings of length 20.

The ARRAY keyword is optional in PL/I, the parenthesised dimension may be placed
immediately after the identifier, e.g. DCL A(10) FIXED BIN(31), which is
syntactically equivalent to DCL ARRAY A(10) FIXED BIN(31).

